  "summary": "Your forehand is a loop against backspin, and the main problem is that you stand too tall. Your legs aren't helping lift the ball.\n\n**Best rallies:** I found 18 rallies in the match and made a 42-second highlight reel of your 5 longest. The best one ran 13 shots.\n\n**Your forehand:** I measured three of your forehands and compared them with the right-handed pros whose build and style are closest to yours. You're closest to Ma Long, then Harimoto and Fan Zhendong. You also have a pro comparison video and a breakdown video of your own stroke with 3 coaching cards.\n\n**What to work on first:**\n1. **Sit lower.** Your knees are almost straight when you hit the ball, about 30\u00b0 straighter than Fan Zhendong's. Against backspin, the lift should come from your legs. Aim for a clearly bent knee as you hit.\n2. **Finish high.** Your bat stops around chest height. Harimoto's arm finishes about 47\u00b0 higher. Swing up to forehead height so the ball clears the net instead of going into it.\n3. **Bend your elbow more.** Your arm is a bit stiff when you hit, about 17\u00b0 straighter than Fan Zhendong's. A looser elbow lets your forearm snap and brush the ball.\n\n**Keep:** your wide stance (about twice your shoulder width) is a good base.\n\n**Drill:** do 3 sets of 20 shadow forehand loops from a low squat, holding the finish each time with the bat at your forehead.\n\nThe knee and elbow targets on the cards (about 125\u00b0 for the knee and 105\u00b0 for the elbow) are rough estimates. They're your angles adjusted by the gaps to Fan Zhendong, not exact pro figures.",
